        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-22",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "%% Internal connections within Turtledb"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-23",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "B --> C"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-24",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "B -.-> C"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-25",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "C --> D"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "F --> G"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-27",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "F --> D"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-28",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "G --> B"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-29",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "D --> E"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "%% Main data flow"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A --> B"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-32",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "H --> B"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-33",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "E --> A"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-34",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "%% Labels for key connections"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-35",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "B -->|\"Semantic Query Vector\"| C"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-36",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "B -->|\"Identified Keywords/Concepts\"| C"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "C -->|\"Structured Prolog Query\"| D"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "F -->|\"Node/Edge Types & Semantic Descriptions\"| G"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-39",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "F -->|\"Formal Rules\"| D"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-40",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "```"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-41",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Explanation of the Diagram:**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-42",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **User Input (A):** The process starts with a natural language query from the user in the UI."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-43",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **NLP Pre-processor & Embedder (B):**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-44",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- This is the first component within Turtledb's JSR package that processes the natural language."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-45",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- It takes the raw query and converts it into a numerical vector (a \"Semantic Query Vector\") using embedding models."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-46",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- It also attempts to identify key concepts or keywords within the query (e.g., \"people,\" \"talked about,\" \"new feature\")."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-47",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Crucially, it uses the \"Schema Embedder\" (G) and potentially an \"External Language Model\" (H) to understand the *meaning* of the user's words in relation to your defined schema."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-48",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. **Schema Embedder (G):**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-49",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- This component takes your \"User-Defined Schema/Config\" (F) which contains node types (e.g., `member`, `post`) and edge types (e.g., `comments_on`), along with their associated \"Semantic Descriptions\" (e.g., `member` described as \"a person affiliated with the application\")."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-50",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- It generates embeddings for each of these schema elements, allowing them to be semantically compared to the user's query."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-51",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. **Query Mapper & Resolver Orchestrator (C):**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-52",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- This is the brain of the prolog-inspired resolver."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-53",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- It receives the \"Semantic Query Vector\" and identified keywords from (B)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-54",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Its primary role is to map the natural language intent to your formal \"User-Defined Schema/Config\" (F). It does this by finding the most semantically similar node and edge types."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-55",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Once mapped, it constructs a \"Structured Prolog Query\" (or a logic program query) using the formal rules defined in your schema. This query will have variables (e.g., `?Member`, `?Post`) that prolog will attempt to bind."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-56",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5. **Prolog-inspired Logic Engine (D):**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-57",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- This is where the actual deduction happens."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-58",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- It takes the \"Structured Prolog Query\" from (C) and the \"Formal Rules\" from the \"User-Defined Schema/Config\" (F). These rules define how relationships are traversed and what constitutes valid deductions (e.g., `member_comments_on_post(Member, Post) :- member_node(Member), comments_on_edge(Member, Post)`)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-59",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- It queries the \"Graph Store (Nodes & Edges)\" (E) to find matching data based on the logical program. This simulates prolog's unification and backtracking."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-60",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "6. **Graph Store (Nodes & Edges) (E):**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-61",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- This is your actual data, stored as nodes and edges, as you've designed in your `graph-model.md`."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-62",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- It's the source of truth that the \"Prolog-inspired Logic Engine\" queries."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-63",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "7. **External Language Models (H - Optional):**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-64",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- This represents a potential integration with external services (like OpenAI, Hugging Face, or local LLMs) for more sophisticated embeddings, general knowledge, or even generating the initial structured query for simple cases. It provides semantic context beyond what's explicitly defined in your local schema."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-65",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "8. **Query Results:** The structured graph data matching the query is returned back to the UI (A)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-66",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This diagram illustrates a powerful synergy: semantic embeddings provide the fuzziness and understanding needed for natural language, while the prolog-inspired logic engine provides the precision and deduction capabilities to execute the query against your structured graph data. The user-defined schema acts as the crucial bridge, making the entire system flexible and domain-agnostic."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}